# GitHub issues

Use `gh` for every GitHub issue/PR/check. Do not invent issues in chat — create them on GitHub when asked.

Issue templates are **markdown** (not YAML forms) so `gh issue create --template` works. The agent should still pass `--body` with filled-in content; `--template` plus `--body` cannot be combined.

## One-time labels

If `gh issue create --label` fails because a label is missing:

```bash
make gh-labels
```

## Create an issue

Fill real content (never leave HTML comments or empty sections). Then:

```bash
gh issue create \
  --title "[bug] Short actionable title" \
  --label bug \
  --body "$(cat <<'EOF'
## Summary
One or two sentences.

## Steps to Reproduce
1. …
2. …

## Expected Behavior
…

## Actual Behavior
…

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Bug is fixed
- [ ] Regression covered (if applicable)

## Notes
Optional.
EOF
)"
```

| Type in title | `--label` |
|---------------|-----------|
| `[bug]` | `bug` |
| `[feature]` | `enhancement` |
| `[chore]` | `chore` |
| `[docs]` | `documentation` |
| `[test]` | `test` |

## Title and body

- Title: `[type] Short, actionable title` — types: `feature`, `bug`, `chore`, `docs`, `test`
- English only
- Acceptance criteria must be checkboxes
- No story points, estimates, or time fields
- Keep it short

Body shape (must match the template):

```markdown
## Summary
…

## Context
Why this exists. Link related issues or docs.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] Testable outcome 1
- [ ] Testable outcome 2

## Notes
Optional constraints or open questions.
```

Bug reports add **Steps to Reproduce**, **Expected Behavior**, **Actual Behavior** instead of Context.

## After create

Return the issue URL. Do not create issues unless the user asked.
